course: world
lesson: 126
title: "In the olden days": Grandad's 1990
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To a child, anything before they were born feels ancient, but dates let you check. 1990 is 36 years before 2026, about 3 and a half times a 10-year-old's life, while the Middle Ages ended about 500 years ago. "Back in the day" and "in my day" mean "when I was young", so the day changes with whoever says it.
explain: Many everyday things are new. The World Wide Web went public in 1991, YouTube started in 2005, and smartphones became common after 2007. When Grandad was 10, in 1980, ordinary families had no internet at all; in the 1990s it came down the phone line through a screeching dial-up modem, and nobody could use the house phone while you were online.
explain: Every time had its own exciting tech. The Game Boy of 1989 had a small greenish screen and kids loved it as much as kids love new consoles now; music came on cassette tapes, and people wound the tape back with a pencil to save the batteries.
explain: Old things also look old because they have aged: photos on film fade and go orange. Prices rise over the years, which is called inflation, so a 10p bag of sweets now costs £1, but people's pay was lower back then too.
explain: Family dates often line up with history: Ukraine became independent on 24 August 1991, and a grandad who watched the Moon landing on TV at 7, in 1969, was born in about 1962.
fact: 1990 was 36 years before 2026; the Middle Ages, in Europe roughly the years 500 to 1500, ended about 500 years ago.
fact: The Game Boy came out in 1989, 37 years before 2026.
fact: In the 1990s home internet used the phone line through a dial-up modem, which could take about 30 seconds to connect with beeps and screeches and was far too slow for watching films.
fact: The World Wide Web went public in 1991, YouTube started in 2005 and smartphones became common after 2007.
fact: Ukraine declared independence on 24 August 1991, 35 years before 2026.
fact: Prices rising over the years is called inflation; £1 is 100p, which is 10 times 10p.
fact: Old photos were taken on film, and their colours fade over time, often going orange or yellow.
fact: Someone born in 1989 was born in the 1900s, the 20th century.
fact: The first Moon landing was on 20 July 1969; someone who was 7 then was born in about 1962 and is about 64 in 2026.
fact: Cassettes were tapes for music, common from the 1970s to the 1990s.
fact: "Back in the day" and "in my day" mean "in the past, when I was young".
exercise: How long before 2026 was 1990? | 36 years | 26 years | 46 years | 360 years
exercise: When did the Middle Ages end, roughly? | About 500 years ago | About 36 years ago | About 50 years ago | About 5,000 years ago
exercise: When did the Game Boy come out? | 1989 | 1969 | 2005 | 1999
exercise: How did home internet connect in the 1990s? | Through the phone line, with a dial-up modem | Through a big satellite dish fixed to every house | Through the TV aerial up on the roof of the house | Through a radio tower standing in each street
exercise: Why couldn't anyone use the house phone while you were online in the 1990s? | The internet used the same phone line | Phones were banned during internet time | The modem made the phone too hot | Calls cost too much at night
exercise: When did the World Wide Web go public? | 1991 | 1969 | 2005 | 1975
exercise: When did YouTube start? | 2005 | 1991 | 1980 | 2015
exercise: When did Ukraine declare independence? | 24 August 1991 | 24 August 1918 | 1 January 2000 | 20 July 1969
exercise: A bag of sweets cost 10p and now costs £1. How many times more is that? | 10 | 100 | 90 | 2
exercise: What is inflation? | Prices rising over time | Prices falling over time | Money changing colour | Shops closing early
exercise: Why do old photos often look orange? | The colours on the film fade over time | People in the 1970s really were a bit orange | Cameras back then could only see the colour orange | The Sun shone more orange in those days
exercise: Josh's mum was born in 1989. Which century is that? | The 20th century | The 19th century | The 21st century | The 18th century
exercise: Grandad was 7 when he watched the Moon landing in 1969. In about which year was he born? | 1962 | 1976 | 1969 | 1952
exercise: What were cassettes? | Tapes for playing music | Small video games | Early mobile phones | Discs for photos
exercise: What does "back in the day" mean? | In the past, when the speaker was young | Exactly one day ago | In the Middle Ages | Behind the day's timetable
test: Grandad: "No phones in our pockets in 1990." Kai: "So, like, the Middle Ages, then." Fix it. | 1990 was 36 years ago; the Middle Ages ended about 500 years ago | Kai's right: 1990 was in the Middle Ages | 1990 was about 1,000 years ago | The Middle Ages ended in 1990
test: Grandad: "Green and black screen, and we loved it, you ungrateful lot!" Which machine is he on about? | The Game Boy, from 1989 | The smartphone, from 2007 | A tablet, from 2010 | YouTube, from 2005
test: Mum: "Beep, screech, kshhh, that was the internet connecting." Ava: "Your WiFi screamed? I'm crying." ("I'm crying" means I'm laughing so much.) What made the noise? | A dial-up modem sending signals down the phone line | The computer's fan breaking down | A WiFi router from the 1990s that was starting to break down | A radio playing in the next room
test: Ella: "Was there YouTube when you were 10, Grandad?" He was 10 in 1980. What's right? | No; the web went public in 1991 and YouTube started in 2005 | Yes, but only in black and white | Yes, YouTube started in 1975 | No, but everyone had smartphones
test: Taras: "Dad was born in 1991. So Ukraine and Dad are the same age?" Is he right? | Yes, Ukraine declared independence in August 1991 | No, Ukraine only became independent much later, in 2001 | No, Ukraine became independent long before, in 1969 | No, Ukraine is 1,544 years old, the same age as Kyiv
test: Grandad, a proper Geordie (from Newcastle): "Sweets were 10p, man, now they're a quid, it's daylight robbery!" ("A quid" means £1.) What's the honest reply? | Prices rise over time, but people's pay was lower then too | The shop is cheating him out of 90p | Sweets cost 100 times more now | Pennies were worth less in the old days, so sweets were cheaper still
test: Lily: "Why's this 1975 photo all orange and fuzzy? Was the world orange?" | The film has faded with age | Everything in 1975 was painted orange | Cameras then added orange on purpose | The Sun was redder in the 1970s
test: Josh: "Mum, were you alive in the 1900s?" She sighs: "Born 1989, so technically yes. Cheers." What's true? | 1989 is in the 1900s, the 20th century | 1989 is in the 1800s | 1989 is in the 21st century | The 1900s ended in 1950
test: Grandad: "Watched the Moon landing at 7, back in '69." Leo: "So how old are you now?" It's 2026. | About 64 | About 57 | About 76 | About 69
test: Ava's dad: "Back in the day, we rewound cassettes with a pencil." Ava: "Back in what day?" What does he mean? | When he was young, before music was on phones | One special day in history | Yesterday, before school | In the Middle Ages
test: What would happen if you tried to watch a film online at home in 1995? | You couldn't really: dial-up was far too slow | It would load in a second, just like it does now | It would work fine, but only on the big family TV | It would be completely free and start instantly
exam: Ascertain the interval elapsed between the year 1990 and the year 2026. | Thirty-six years | Twenty-six years | Forty-six years | Three hundred and sixty years
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a household telephone line could not be employed for calls during an internet session in the 1990s. | The connection to the internet occupied that same telephone line | Telephone calls were prohibited by law during such sessions | The modem disabled all telephones within the district | Calls could be made only after midnight
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon designated inflation? | A general increase in prices over a period of time | A general reduction in prices over a period of time | The printing of banknotes in brighter colours | The closure of shops at an earlier hour
exam: Given that a person was seven years of age at the first Moon landing in 1969, determine that person's approximate age in the year 2026. | Sixty-four years | Fifty-seven years | Seventy-six years | Sixty-nine years
